      "math_academy_id": "e-13046",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\dfrac{2^3}{2^5}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To divide powers of the same base, we subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2^3}{2^5} = 2^{3-5} = 2^{-2}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $2^{-2},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n2^{-2}  = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$",
